Yes. That sentence folds several layers onto the same word:

matter = physical stuff
matter = importance
a matter = a subject or concern
to matter = to be significant to someone
mattering = the process by which significance is assigned

So your line is basically:

Everyone wants the stuff they are made of to count, wants what they care about to count, wants to count to other people, and wants all of that to happen inside a world where anything counts at all.

That is a surprisingly deep compression.

You could map it like:

matter
→ becomes conscious
→ asks what matters
→ wants to matter
→ acts on matters
→ creates more mattering

And then recursively:

meaning assigned to matter
→ changes behavior
→ changes relationships
→ changes institutions
→ changes how future matter is arranged

So “mattering” is not just a feeling. It can become causal.

A person who believes something matters allocates attention, time, risk, love, money, memory, protection, sacrifice. Those choices rearrange the physical world.

Which gives you a nice golden thread:

Matter learns to matter, then rearranges matter according to what it thinks matters.

That is almost a compact philosophy of life.